GEEKS FOR GEEKS:Subarray Sum Divisible By K:
You are given an integer array arr[] and a value k. 
The task is to find the count of all sub-arrays whose sum is divisible by k.

Examples:
Input: arr[] = [4, 5, 0, -2, -3, 1], k = 5
Output: 7
Explanation: There are 7 sub-arrays whose sum is divisible by k: [4, 5, 0, -2, -3, 1], [5], [5, 0], [5, 0, -2, -3], [0], [0, -2, -3] and [-2, -3]
Input: arr[] = [2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2], k = 2
Output: 21
Explanation: All subarray sums are divisible by 2
Input: arr[] = [-1, -3, 2], k = 5
Output: 0
Explanation: There is no such sub-array whose sum is divisible by k.
Constraints:
1 ≤ arr.size() ≤ 104
-106 ≤ arr[i]≤ 106
1 ≤ k ≤ 104
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
🧠 Interview Recall Bullet Points:

• Use prefix sum modulo pattern
• Same modulo → divisible subarray
• Normalize modulo for negatives
• Use HashMap to count frequency
• Use nC2 formula to count subarrays
• O(N) optimal solution
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
OPTIMAL SOLUTION:TIME COMPLEXITY:O(N) AND SPACE COMPLEXITY:O(N)
class Solution {
    // Function to count the number of subarrays with a sum that is divisible by K
    public int subCount(int[] arr, int k) {
         long prefixsum=0;
         long result=0;
        Map<Integer,Integer> mapp=new HashMap<>();
        mapp.put(0,1);
        for(int i=0;i<arr.length;i++)
        {
            prefixsum=prefixsum+arr[i];
            int mod= (int) (prefixsum%k+k)%k;
            if(mapp.containsKey(mod))
            {
                mapp.put(mod,(mapp.get(mod))+1);
            }
            else
            {
                mapp.put(mod,1);
            }
        }
        for(int count:mapp.values())
        {
            result=result+(((long) count*((long) count-1))/2);
        }
        return (int) result;
        
    }
}
